//! `Field<p>`: the general, CTFE-derived Montgomery modular-multiplication
//! engine. Rollcall precision axis, P2 (PRECISION_TYPES_RESEARCH.md P2;
//! ROLLCALL_GOAL.md "Precision axis": "`Field<p>` proven == both BN254
//! fixtures limb-for-limb, retiring gen_field_mul.py and unifying the
//! 4-backend prover"). `Field<p>`'s multiply is the linchpin: it is both the
//! prover's element type and the gate for the Conal Merkle slice.
//!
//! THE PRINCIPLE (ROLLCALL_GOAL.md): this file is authored ONCE, generic over
//! the limb count `const L: usize` and a `Modulus<L>` instance (the trait
//! carries the prime's limbs, exactly PRECISION_TYPES_RESEARCH.md P2's
//! prescription: "`Montgomery` parameter block as trait consts"). BN254 Fr
//! (`bn254_fr.fe`) is the first, and only required, instantiation; any other
//! prime specializes the SAME code by picking `L` and a `Modulus<L>` impl.
//! `mu` (the CIOS Montgomery constant) is not hand-supplied: it is DERIVED
//! from the modulus's low limb by a real CTFE computation (`mont_mu`, a
//! 2-adic Newton iteration), and `bn254_fr.fe` proves the derivation correct
//! with a `static_assert` against the known constant, mirroring how
//! `gen_field_mul.py` computed `mu` OUTSIDE Fe -- here the compiler computes
//! it.
//!
//! REPRESENTATION AND THE BALANCED-TREE MANDATE (read before touching the
//! shape; mirrors the `Expansion<N>` module doc in `lib.fe`, same reasoned
//! exception, different root cause). `Limbs<L>` below is a LINEAR chain
//! (`LimbLast | LimbMore<Limbs<{L-1}>>`), not a balanced binary tree, and
//! this is deliberate:
//!
//!   1. CIOS Montgomery reduction has a genuine sequential CARRY-PROPAGATION
//!      dependency: each limb position's result depends on the carry
//!      produced by the position below it (`Mac`'s `carry_in`/`carry_out`
//!      threading), exactly the species of position-sensitive fold
//!      `Expansion<N>`'s renormalization chain is (there each level threads
//!      a `corr` value; here each level threads a carry). A ripple-carry
//!      dependency is NOT associative: you cannot compute limb k's carry
//!      without first resolving limbs 0..k-1, so a balanced split cannot
//!      reassociate this fold without a fundamentally different technique
//!      (carry-save / redundant representations, deferred, see below).
//!   2. What the P0 mandate's balanced-tree rule DOES bear on here, and is
//!      honored: the SCHOOLBOOK CONVOLUTION underlying a schoolbook multiply
//!      (`sum over i+j=k of a[i]*b[j]`, before any carry normalization) IS
//!      associative/commutative as pure integer arithmetic, so CIOS's whole
//!      *raison d'etre* is precisely to AVOID materializing that O(L^2)-term
//!      sum by interleaving reduction row-by-row; this file follows that
//!      same choice (CIOS, not a naive convolution-then-reduce), so there is
//!      no separate associative fold sitting inside `mul` to balance. The
//!      mandate's actual target (large-N shapes consumed by a trait fold
//!      where the fold genuinely IS associative, e.g. Poseidon's MDS
//!      contraction or a Merkle tree's combine) is untouched by this file.
//!   3. The measured wall is N=255 for a linear chain under a trait fold (P0
//!      Probe 1). BN254 Fr needs `L=20` (`Limbs<20>`) and the WORKING width
//!      `L+2=22` (`Pad2::Out`, see below): both far below the wall, and in
//!      the same safe range P0 Probe 3 and `Expansion<N>` already exercised.
//!
//! WORKING WIDTH (`Pad2`): CIOS needs 2 extra headroom limbs beyond the
//! logical `L` during each row (the multiply-accumulate and reduce-accumulate
//! carries can spill past position `L-1`), exactly as the existing kernels'
//! own `t` arrays are `L+2` long (`field_mul_bn254_fr_loop.fe`). Rather than
//! replicate that kernel's own shifted-index bookkeeping, this file reduces
//! each CIOS row to ONE UNIFORM identity, `t := (t + a*b_i + m*p) / base`,
//! computed as two full-width `Mac` passes over the padded `L+2` width
//! followed by an EXACT division by `base` (dropping the guaranteed-zero low
//! limb, i.e. `ShiftAppend`). This is mathematically equivalent to the
//! existing kernels' piecewise shift (REDC has a UNIQUE correct output for
//! given operands, unlike float expansions there is no op-order-dependent
//! rounding to match), verified independently by the bit-identical oracle
//! (`crates/codegen/tests/precision_field_bn254fr_oracle.rs`), not merely
//! trusted by construction.
//!
//! `Field<p>`'s canonical VALUE representation is the WORKING width itself
//! (`<Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out`, i.e. `Limbs<{L+2}>`, with the top 2 limbs
//! always zero for an in-range element): `mul` takes logical-width operands
//! (`Limbs<L>`, what a caller/oracle harness naturally holds) and returns the
//! padded width. A generic "drop the last 2 limbs" combinator was attempted
//! and hits a genuine compiler limitation (see the task report): projecting
//! an associated type through an ABSTRACT (not-yet-concrete) recursive bound
//! -- `<More<T> as DropLast>::Out` for free `T` -- fails kind-checking
//! (`expected a fully-applied type (kind *) here`), while the SAME shape
//! projected through a bound on a bare type parameter (`Pad2`'s `T::Out`, or
//! `<Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out` for an abstract `const L`) works fine. Rather
//! than force a workaround, this file keeps the padded width as the return
//! type (mathematically sound: the top 2 limbs are always zero for any valid
//! element) and lets concrete call sites (fixed `L`) extract individual
//! limbs by direct, ground field access, exactly the same mechanism the
//! oracle harness already needs for its own per-limb wasm export ABI.

use core::marker::Copy

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Limb storage: a linear, LEAST-SIGNIFICANT-LIMB-FIRST HList (`lead` = limb 0).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub struct LimbLast { pub value: u32 }
pub struct LimbMore<T> { pub lead: u32, pub tail: T }
impl Copy for LimbLast {}
impl<T: Copy> Copy for LimbMore<T> {}

/// `Limbs<L>`: `L` limbs, LSB-first. `Limbs<0>` and `Limbs<1>` both normalize
/// to `LimbLast` (a single limb is its own base case; mirrors `Expansion<N>`).
pub recursive type fn Limbs<const L: usize>() -> (*) {
    match L {
        0 => LimbLast
        1 => LimbLast
        _ => LimbMore<Limbs<{L - 1}>>
    }
}

/// The one portable limb width (PRECISION_TYPES_RESEARCH.md section 4's limb
/// width doctrine: portable 13 by default on every backend, digest-identical;
/// a per-backend-Word fast tier is a later, measurement-gated opt-in, not
/// built here). 13-bit limbs in u32 words is the only sound representation
/// under browser WebGPU (no mul-hi, no u64; the ZPrize WebGPU-MSM house
/// choice), exactly `field_mul_bn254_fr.fe`'s own convention.
pub const LIMB_BITS: u32 = 13
pub const LIMB_BASE: u32 = 8192

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The shared branch-free combinators (each a structural trait fold over
// `Limbs<L>`, the same species of generic recursion `Expansion<N>`'s `Exp`
// trait uses, specialized to integer carry/borrow arithmetic).
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The least-significant limb (needed to compute the CIOS quotient digit `m`).
pub trait Head { fn head(self) -> u32 }
impl Head for LimbLast { fn head(self) -> u32 { self.value } }
impl<T: Copy> Head for LimbMore<T> { fn head(self) -> u32 { self.lead } }

/// All-zero limbs (the CIOS accumulator's initial state).
pub trait Zero { fn zero() -> Self }
impl Zero for LimbLast { fn zero() -> LimbLast { LimbLast { value: 0 } } }
impl<T: Zero> Zero for LimbMore<T> {
    fn zero() -> LimbMore<T> { LimbMore { lead: 0, tail: T::zero() } }
}

/// Branch-free carry-propagating multiply-accumulate: `t := t + x*scalar +
/// carry_in`, per limb, returning the updated limbs and the final carry out
/// of the most significant limb. THE reasoned-linear fold (module doc point
/// 1): each level's `carry_in` is the PRIOR level's `carry_out`, a genuine
/// ripple dependency.
pub trait Mac {
    fn mac(t: Self, x: Self, scalar: u32, carry_in: u32) -> (Self, u32)
}
impl Mac for LimbLast {
    fn mac(t: LimbLast, x: LimbLast, scalar: u32, carry_in: u32) -> (LimbLast, u32) {
        let s: u32 = t.value + x.value * scalar + carry_in
        let c: u32 = s >> LIMB_BITS
        (LimbLast { value: s - c * LIMB_BASE }, c)
    }
}
impl<T: Mac + Copy> Mac for LimbMore<T> {
    fn mac(t: LimbMore<T>, x: LimbMore<T>, scalar: u32, carry_in: u32) -> (LimbMore<T>, u32) {
        let s: u32 = t.lead + x.lead * scalar + carry_in
        let c: u32 = s >> LIMB_BITS
        let lo: u32 = s - c * LIMB_BASE
        let (tail2, c2) = <T as Mac>::mac(t: t.tail, x: x.tail, scalar: scalar, carry_in: c)
        (LimbMore { lead: lo, tail: tail2 }, c2)
    }
}

/// Exact division by `LIMB_BASE`: drop the (guaranteed-zero, by CIOS's
/// construction of `m`) least-significant limb, shift every other limb down
/// one position, and append `v` (always `0` at the call site in `row`) as the
/// new most-significant limb.
pub trait ShiftAppend {
    fn shift_append(t: Self, v: u32) -> Self
}
impl ShiftAppend for LimbLast {
    fn shift_append(t: LimbLast, v: u32) -> LimbLast { LimbLast { value: v } }
}
impl<T: ShiftAppend + Head + Copy> ShiftAppend for LimbMore<T> {
    fn shift_append(t: LimbMore<T>, v: u32) -> LimbMore<T> {
        LimbMore { lead: t.tail.head(), tail: <T as ShiftAppend>::shift_append(t: t.tail, v: v) }
    }
}

/// Zero-extend `Limbs<L>` by exactly 2 limbs (the CIOS working-width
/// headroom): `Limbs<L> -> Limbs<{L+2}>`. Structural recursion bounding the
/// TYPE PARAMETER `T` (not the constructed type `LimbMore<T>`) so the
/// associated-type projection stays over a bare type variable throughout --
/// the shape that is proven to kind-check (see the module doc's account of
/// why the mirror-image truncation was NOT built this way).
pub trait Pad2 {
    type Out
    fn pad2(self) -> Self::Out
}
impl Pad2 for LimbLast {
    type Out = LimbMore<LimbMore<LimbLast>>
    fn pad2(self) -> LimbMore<LimbMore<LimbLast>> {
        LimbMore { lead: self.value, tail: LimbMore { lead: 0, tail: LimbLast { value: 0 } } }
    }
}
impl<T: Pad2 + Copy> Pad2 for LimbMore<T> {
    type Out = LimbMore<T::Out>
    fn pad2(self) -> LimbMore<T::Out> {
        LimbMore { lead: self.lead, tail: self.tail.pad2() }
    }
}

/// Constant-time `t - p - borrow_in`, per limb, base-complement technique
/// (`t[j] + BASE - p[j] - borrow`, borrow bit read off the high side of the
/// limb rather than a compare): verbatim the same idiom
/// `field_mul_bn254_fr_loop.fe`'s own conditional-subtract uses.
pub trait SubBorrow {
    fn sub_borrow(t: Self, p: Self, borrow_in: u32) -> (Self, u32)
}
impl SubBorrow for LimbLast {
    fn sub_borrow(t: LimbLast, p: LimbLast, borrow_in: u32) -> (LimbLast, u32) {
        let dd: u32 = t.value + LIMB_BASE - p.value - borrow_in
        let db: u32 = dd >> LIMB_BITS
        let borrow: u32 = 1 - db
        (LimbLast { value: dd - db * LIMB_BASE }, borrow)
    }
}
impl<T: SubBorrow + Copy> SubBorrow for LimbMore<T> {
    fn sub_borrow(t: LimbMore<T>, p: LimbMore<T>, borrow_in: u32) -> (LimbMore<T>, u32) {
        let dd: u32 = t.lead + LIMB_BASE - p.lead - borrow_in
        let db: u32 = dd >> LIMB_BITS
        let borrow: u32 = 1 - db
        let (tail2, borrow2) = <T as SubBorrow>::sub_borrow(t: t.tail, p: p.tail, borrow_in: borrow)
        (LimbMore { lead: dd - db * LIMB_BASE, tail: tail2 }, borrow2)
    }
}

/// Constant-time per-limb select (mask arithmetic, no branch): `keep_t*t +
/// keep_diff*d`, `keep_t`/`keep_diff` each 0 or 1 and mutually exclusive.
pub trait Select {
    fn select(keep_t: u32, keep_diff: u32, t: Self, d: Self) -> Self
}
impl Select for LimbLast {
    fn select(keep_t: u32, keep_diff: u32, t: LimbLast, d: LimbLast) -> LimbLast {
        LimbLast { value: keep_t * t.value + keep_diff * d.value }
    }
}
impl<T: Select + Copy> Select for LimbMore<T> {
    fn select(keep_t: u32, keep_diff: u32, t: LimbMore<T>, d: LimbMore<T>) -> LimbMore<T> {
        LimbMore {
            lead: keep_t * t.lead + keep_diff * d.lead,
            tail: <T as Select>::select(keep_t: keep_t, keep_diff: keep_diff, t: t.tail, d: d.tail),
        }
    }
}

/// Constant-time canonicalization: `t` if `t < p`, else `t - p` (at most one
/// subtraction, per Montgomery's own bound on CIOS's output range).
pub fn canonicalize<S: SubBorrow + Select + Copy>(_ t: S, _ p: S) -> S {
    let (d, borrow) = <S as SubBorrow>::sub_borrow(t: t, p: p, borrow_in: 0)
    let keep_t: u32 = borrow
    let keep_diff: u32 = 1 - borrow
    <S as Select>::select(keep_t: keep_t, keep_diff: keep_diff, t: t, d: d)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The CIOS row schedule.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One CIOS row over the WORKING width `W` (`= Pad2::Out`, logical limb
/// count + 2 headroom limbs): `t := (t + a*b_i + m*p) / LIMB_BASE`, where `m
/// = (t.head() * mu) mod LIMB_BASE` is chosen so the low limb of `t + m*p`
/// is exactly zero (Montgomery's construction), making the division by
/// `LIMB_BASE` exact.
pub fn row<W: Mac + ShiftAppend + Head + Copy>(_ t: W, _ a: W, _ b_i: u32, _ p: W, _ mu: u32) -> W {
    let (t1, _c1) = <W as Mac>::mac(t: t, x: a, scalar: b_i, carry_in: 0)
    let m0: u32 = t1.head() * mu
    let m: u32 = m0 - (m0 >> LIMB_BITS) * LIMB_BASE
    let (t2, _c2) = <W as Mac>::mac(t: t1, x: p, scalar: m, carry_in: 0)
    <W as ShiftAppend>::shift_append(t: t2, v: 0)
}

/// The OUTER row driver: run exactly `L` CIOS rows, one per limb of `b`
/// (`Self`), each row folding into the fixed working-width accumulator `W`.
/// `Self` (b's remaining rows) shrinks each level; `W` (the CIOS state,
/// `a`, `p`) does NOT, hence the trait's own generic `W` parameter, resolved
/// independently of `Self`'s structural depth at every level (proven not to
/// hit a coherence/overlap wall in the task's incremental probes).
pub trait Rows<W> {
    fn rows(b: Self, t: W, a: W, p: W, mu: u32) -> W
}
impl<W: Mac + ShiftAppend + Head + Copy> Rows<W> for LimbLast {
    fn rows(b: LimbLast, t: W, a: W, p: W, mu: u32) -> W {
        row<W>(t, a, b.value, p, mu)
    }
}
impl<T: Rows<W> + Copy, W: Mac + ShiftAppend + Head + Copy> Rows<W> for LimbMore<T> {
    fn rows(b: LimbMore<T>, t: W, a: W, p: W, mu: u32) -> W {
        let t1: W = row<W>(t, a, b.lead, p, mu)
        <T as Rows<W>>::rows(b: b.tail, t: t1, a: a, p: p, mu: mu)
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Montgomery parameter block, as trait consts (PRECISION_TYPES_RESEARCH.md
// P2's own prescription), and the CTFE derivation of `mu` from `p`'s low limb.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// 2-adic Newton iteration for `p0^-1 mod 2^bits`: `x_{k+1} = x_k*(2 -
/// p0*x_k) mod 2^bits`, doubling correct bits each step from `x0=1` (correct
/// mod 2, since `p0` is odd for any prime modulus > 2). `mu = -x mod
/// 2^bits`. Six iterations converges (and stays fixed) well past `bits=13`;
/// every subtraction is restructured to add `base` first so CTFE's CHECKED
/// arithmetic never sees an intermediate underflow.
pub const fn mont_mu(_ p0: u32, _ bits: u32) -> u32 {
    let base: u32 = 1 << bits
    let mask: u32 = base - 1
    let mut x: u32 = 1
    let mut i: u32 = 0
    while i < 6 {
        let px: u32 = (p0 * x) & mask
        let t: u32 = (base + 2 - px) & mask
        x = (x * t) & mask
        i = i + 1
    }
    (base - x) & mask
}

/// The Montgomery parameter block for a prime `p`, `L` limbs of `LIMB_BITS`
/// each. `mu()` is expected to equal `mont_mu(p_limbs().head(), LIMB_BITS)`;
/// instances prove this with a top-level `static_assert` (see `bn254_fr.fe`)
/// rather than the trait deriving it inline, since a generic function body
/// cannot fold a runtime call to a `const fn` into a compile-time constant
/// without a literal in the generated kernel -- the `static_assert` gives
/// the same CTFE-derivation guarantee (the compiler verifies the literal
/// against the real computation) with zero runtime cost in the specialized
/// kernel.
pub trait Modulus<const L: usize> {
    fn p_limbs() -> Limbs<L>
    fn mu() -> u32
}

/// `Field<p>::mul`: the general entry point. `L` and `M` are explicit at
/// every call site (mirrors `Expansion<N>`'s own doctrine: consts are not
/// inferred from a concrete argument by this compiler). Returns the WORKING
/// width (`Pad2::Out`, i.e. `Limbs<{L+2}>`, top 2 limbs always zero); see the
/// module doc for why.
pub fn mul<const L: usize, M: Modulus<L>>(_ a: Limbs<L>, _ b: Limbs<L>) -> <Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out
    where Limbs<L>: Pad2 + Rows< <Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out > + Copy,
          <Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out: Mac + ShiftAppend + Head + Zero + SubBorrow + Select + Copy,
{
    let a2 = a.pad2()
    let p: Limbs<L> = M::p_limbs()
    let p2 = p.pad2()
    let t0 = <<Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out as Zero>::zero()
    let t_final = <Limbs<L> as Rows< <Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out > >::rows(
        b: b, t: t0, a: a2, p: p2, mu: M::mu(),
    )
    canonicalize< <Limbs<L> as Pad2>::Out >(t_final, p2)
}
